upsert_data (ghost_dom.rs:303-310) is called every 10 frames with 4 temporary Strings created via to_string()/format!():
  state.frame_count.to_string()   // ~20 bytes
  format!("{:.1}", state.fps)     // ~8 bytes  
  state.width.to_string()          // ~4 bytes
  state.height.to_string()         // ~4 bytes

That's 4 heap allocations (~36 bytes) × 6 calls/sec = 24 allocs/sec. Each allocation hits the WASM linear memory allocator (dlmalloc), which is slower than native malloc.

Fix: Add a reusable String buffer to GhostDom:
  fmt_buf: String,  // capacity ~64 bytes, never freed

Then use write!() to format into the buffer:
  use std::fmt::Write;
  self.fmt_buf.clear();
  write!(self.fmt_buf, "{}", state.frame_count).unwrap();
  let _ = el.set_attribute("data-frame-count", &self.fmt_buf);
  // repeat for each attr

This eliminates all 4 allocations per call. The buffer stays allocated at its high-water mark (~20 bytes) and just gets reused.

Priority is low because 24 allocs/sec at ~36 bytes each is genuinely negligible. This becomes meaningful if upsert_data is called more frequently in the future (e.g., per-frame instead of every 10 frames) or if more fields are added.

