description:
Currently ghost_dom.sync() compares 8 independent field groups to detect changes. If AutomationState derived PartialEq, a single comparison could short-circuit the entire sync when nothing changed (the common case at 60fps).

AutomationState contains: bool, u64, f32, u32, String, Option<String>, Vec<String>, Vec<ParameterInfo>. All are PartialEq-capable. ParameterInfo (automation.rs:77-82) contains String and bool — also trivially PartialEq.

Add derives:
  #[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Default, PartialEq)]
  pub struct AutomationState { ... }

  #[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, PartialEq)]
  pub struct ParameterInfo { ... }

Then sync() gains an early exit:
  pub fn sync(&mut self, state: &AutomationState) {
      if !first && self.previous.as_ref() == Some(state) { return; }
      // ... existing field-level logic ...
  }

This makes the steady-state hot path (60fps, nothing changing) a single pointer comparison + memcmp, instead of 8 separate field comparisons with short-circuit evaluation. The field-level comparisons still run when something DID change, to determine which DOM sections need updating.

Note: f32 PartialEq uses bitwise comparison (NaN != NaN), which is fine for fps — it's never NaN in practice, and the bucketing logic handles floating-point jitter anyway.

Depends on: the CachedState→previous snapshot refactor (the early exit needs the previous AutomationState).

get_or_create (ghost_dom.rs:252-267) returns a web_sys::Element but doesn't indicate whether it was newly created or retrieved from cache. Every caller then unconditionally sets both immutable attrs (role, data-widget-type, aria-label, tabindex) and dynamic attrs (data-value, aria-disabled, aria-selected).

For existing elements, the immutable attr sets are redundant DOM calls. While browsers optimize setAttribute to no-op when value is unchanged (no reflow), this:
(a) wastes ~4 JS interop round-trips per element per state change
(b) obscures intent — reader can't tell which attrs are one-time vs dynamic
(c) would trigger MutationObservers if any were attached (automation tools sometimes use these)

Fix: Change get_or_create to return (Element, bool) where bool = true means newly created. Each helper then guards immutable attrs behind the bool:

  fn get_or_create(&mut self, id: &str, tag: &str) -> (web_sys::Element, bool) {
      if let Some(el) = self.nodes.get(id) {
          return (el.clone(), false);
      }
      // ... create and insert ...
      (el, true)
  }

  fn upsert_status(&mut self, id: &str, label: &str, value: &str) {
      let (el, created) = self.get_or_create(id, "div");
      if created {
          let _ = el.set_attribute("role", "status");
          let _ = el.set_attribute("data-widget-type", widget_type::STATUS);
      }
      // Dynamic attrs always set:
      let _ = el.set_attribute("aria-label", label);
      let _ = el.set_attribute("data-value", value);
      el.set_text_content(Some(&format!("{label}: {value}")));
  }

Impact: Eliminates ~40% of set_attribute calls on existing elements. Most visible during streaming when frame_stats updates every 10 frames — currently sets 6 attrs but only 4 change.
